# EVA WIRING - a built ruin is somewhere the crew can go, with nothing written per mission.
#
# What a mission used to copy out of Storm's Beacon, once: a ruin is offered to the crew
# when their ship is at its door, the crew who go inside pick up that ruin's side stories,
# and taking the piece is a quest beat. The work is the library's
# (`procedural/eva_relics.py`, `amd_relics.py`); this file only ROUTES, because a signal
# needs a route and routes are MAST.
#
# ROUTES ONLY, AND EVERY ONE RUNS ONCE, ON THE SERVER. Each of these offers, grants, opens
# or sends, and a plain `//signal` runs once per connected console - five consoles would
# offer the ruin five times and hand out five copies of every story. So the signal routes
# are `//shared/signal`, and the one `//damage/destroy` route is server-run by
# construction: a lifetime route is registered once and runs on the server's task,
# whatever the console count.
#
# A MISSION THAT STILL DOES THIS ITSELF IS HARMLESS. Every call below is identity, not a
# latch: a second `eva_relic_open` for the ruin already on offer changes nothing, a story
# already granted is left where it went, and a quest signal a ruin sent is not sent again.
#
# TO WIRE BY HAND instead, put `eva_relics_auto(False)` at the top of the story and call
# `eva_relic_open(key)` / `eva_relic_close(key)` where the story wants them.

# THE RUNTIME TWIN of this file being loaded. A mission, or a test, that wants to know
# whether ruins wire themselves declares `default shared BOARDING_EVA_WIRING = False` and
# reads it - order-safe whichever top level runs first, because the other is a `default`.
shared BOARDING_EVA_WIRING = True


# A ruin was built. Nothing is offered HERE: the offer follows the ship, so this starts
# the pass that makes it (once - asking twice watches once) and runs it now, for a ruin
# built with the ship already at its door.
//shared/signal/relic_built
    ->END if not eva_relics_auto()
    eva_relics_watch()
    eva_relics_pass()
    ->END


# The crew who go inside pick up that ruin's side stories - each crew member the one for
# their job. `relic_section` reads the relic's OWN file, so one ruin's stories go to the
# crew in that ruin and nobody else. None when the file has no such section, which grants
# nothing and is not an error.
//shared/signal/eva_went_out
    ->END if EVA_RELIC is None
    boarding_quests_grant(relic_section(EVA_RELIC, "side_stories"))
    ->END


# Once the crew has reached the first place with something to say, the party is settled:
# a story nobody aboard has the job for goes to all of them rather than to nobody.
//shared/signal/eva_place_scene
    ->END if EVA_RELIC is None
    boarding_quests_open_unclaimed(relic_section(EVA_RELIC, "side_stories"))
    ->END


# TAKING THE PIECE IS A STORY BEAT: `<relic key>_taken`. Carried out on a tether it is
# seen leaving the ruin by the library's own tick. REELED IN from a suit it is collected -
# and deleted - while still inside, so it never leaves anything; this is that half.
# Handed every pickup: one that is nobody's piece is a no-op.
//shared/signal/item_collected
    # `default`, because the pickup's id joined this signal later than its key did. An
    # items addon from before that still emits without it, and reading a name the signal
    # did not carry ends the handler without a word.
    default key = None
    default item_id = None
    relic_piece_collected(key, item_id)
    ->END


# A SHIP'S BEAMS OPEN A BARRIER TOO. A shut barrier is a real object (`relic_barrier`, 40
# hull) so that a weapon has something to hit; a suit's cutting beam opens it through the
# library, and this is the other way in - the ship outside shot it away. The library opens
# the way through and sends `<barrier key>_opened`, once, exactly as when a suit cuts it.
#
# Conditioned on the role, so nothing else's destruction even starts a task here; and the
# call is itself a no-op for an object that is nobody's barrier.
#
# A `Repair:` job is NOT done by being shot. Its marker wears `relic_repair`, never
# `relic_barrier`, and the job is read from the ruin's record rather than from the marker -
# so a marker somebody destroyed leaves a job that still has to be done.
//damage/destroy if has_role(DESTROYED_ID, "relic_barrier")
    relic_barrier_destroyed(DESTROYED_ID)
    ->END


# The marker of a `Repair:` job was destroyed. Nothing is repaired; the library only
# forgets the marker's id, so finishing the job later cannot put away whatever object the
# engine has since handed that id to.
//damage/destroy if has_role(DESTROYED_ID, "relic_repair")
    relic_barrier_destroyed(DESTROYED_ID)
    ->END
